WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.080
- [David] Hello, readers.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.060
Let's argue.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.580
In writing, argument is a kind of fight.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.210
But I think it is unwise

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:13.050
to think of it as a one-way conversation.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.150
The best arguments do not
plunge forward heedlessly.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.440
They do not steamroll opposition.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.220
Rather, they seek out opposing arguments

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.930
and incorporate them.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.670
Good readers pay attention
to how authors acknowledge

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.100
opposing viewpoints in an argument.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.980
Doing this makes it easier to decide

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.750
how well thought out an argument is

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.320
and determine whether the
author is knowledgeable

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.970
and thoughtful in their position.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.820
Now, I do not practice the
martial art of Wing Chun.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.470
But that style of self-defense
is famous for techniques

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.590
that block and strike at the same time.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.100
Your opponent's fist comes in,

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.720
you redirect it away from your body,

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.680
and in that same motion
continue your momentum

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.610
into its own immediate counterstrike.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.970
Acknowledging the existence
of opposing viewpoints

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:01.370
has two main applications,
which are pretty different.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.250
First, maybe you're not on
any side of the argument

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.660
and you wish to straightforwardly
describe the conflict,

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.010
like a journalist reporting on a lawsuit.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:11.640
It's a journalist's responsibility

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.400
to truthfully portray the
full scope of information

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.310
on a topic, bringing
every piece of relevant

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.850
and plausible information to bear.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.220
The author provides equal
time to the disputants

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.230
and tries not to provide
their own opinion here.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.010
That could look like this.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:30.170
Ms. Adeyemi contended that
Mr. Dupont stole the cookies

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.110
from her cookie jar, and that furthermore,

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.340
she had baked those
cookies in the first place,

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.450
thus strengthening her
claim to the said cookies.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:39.750
Of the 24 cookies baked by Ms. Adeyemi,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.340
she alleges that 10 of them
were taken by Mr. Dupont.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.180
Mr. Dupont, however, contended
that as he was the person

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:48.220
who made the cookie jar for Ms. Adeyemi

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.610
in the ceramics class he took,

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:52.240
he is entitled to the occasional cookie.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.480
He does not dispute the 10-cookie figure,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.110
adding only that they were "delicious."

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.110
Judge Huang, upon reviewing
testimony from both parties,

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:01.620
sided with Ms. Adeyemi,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.800
saying that you are not
entitled to a cookie

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.800
just for being a good friend one time,

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.690
and that next time, Mr. Dupont
should ask for permission.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.350
Now, looking at that situation,

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:13.710
my personal opinion is
that it is inappropriate

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:14.950
to steal your friend's cookies.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.700
An opinion that any right-thinking
person should share.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.300
But injecting opinion may not
be appropriate in all cases.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.200
So okay, that's the first case.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.010
Reporting on multiple sides of a dispute

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.470
without getting involved yourself.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:29.610
Now, the second reason,

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.800
the second application for
including opposing viewpoints

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.440
in an argumentative piece of writing

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.240
is that Wing Chun thing, the
block as you strike technique.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:39.620
It makes your argument stronger

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.020
if you anticipate
possible objections to it,

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.190
address them, and strike them down

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:48.000
all the while still making your
argument in the first place.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.090
Let's take this example from the website.

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:53.080
This piece is about the
lost colony at Roanoke.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.380
A bunch of English settlers disappeared

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.240
under uncertain circumstances
for reasons still unknown.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.570
And the author's trying to make the case

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.140
that although theories for
the disappearance exist,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.850
the truth is still beyond our grasp.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.270
So in order to make that case,

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:08.930
they address several different theories

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.123
that attempt to explain the disappearance.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.710
Many people have tried
to solve the mystery

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.280
of the lost colony.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.060
Historians and archeologists
have searched for answers

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.600
for hundreds of years.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.310
Some possible solutions are:

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:25.660
A, Native people killed the
colonists on Roanoke Island.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.400
B, the colonists were all killed

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.070
on Roanoke Island by disease.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.410
C, their town was washed
away by a hurricane,

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:32.550
and the people drowned.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.720
Or D, they all left Roanoke Island

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.310
and went to live in some other place.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.790
Now, watch what the author
does as they systematically

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.010
go through each theory and address it.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.220
I've only excepted the first two,

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.170
so let's look at those first two.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:50.030
A, when John White got back
to Roanoke Island in 1590

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.350
he did not find any bones or bodies

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.200
or any signs of fighting.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.090
Bones last a long time.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.070
If Native people had killed the colonists,

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.930
there would probably
have been some remains

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:01.980
or other signs of violence.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.270
We can guess that the colonists

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:06.060
were not killed on Roanoke Island.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.040
B, when the English
first came to Virginia,

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.790
they're using a 16th-century spelling,

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:14.520
they brought diseases with
them, such as flu and smallpox,

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.460
that were new to the Native communities.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:17.960
They had no immunity against them

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:19.820
and many died very quickly.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:21.390
Letters written by the colonists

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.350
tell us that the English remained healthy.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.970
And remember, no bones or
bodies or graves were found.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:30.330
We can guess that the colonists
did not all die of disease.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.300
So the author's saying that
these events are unlikely,

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.760
taking the theories seriously,

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.030
and then thinking them
through to their conclusions.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:38.730
But demonstrating why they don't seem

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.500
very likely explanations.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.040
Why do they think it's unlikely

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.110
that Native people killed those colonists?

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.653
There aren't any remains
or signs of combat.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.290
Why does it seem unlikely
that they died of diseases?

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:52.270
There are no graves or remains.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.310
The thesis is, nobody knows why.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:57.030
Here are two counters to it
basically saying here's why,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.880
and the author says here's why
these are unlikely to be true

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.580
which weakens those counterarguments

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.620
and strengthens the original.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:05.900
Block, strike.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.940
My overall point is this.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:11.250
Don't ignore the potential
counters to your argument.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.300
Incorporate them, analyze them,

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:16.740
and redirect their energy
into your own position.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:17.870
And when you read,

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.430
notice if the author is
acknowledging counters

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:21.840
or ignoring them.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.520
If they ignore them, you
should be suspicious.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.970
Why are they ignoring them?

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:31.053
If you can answer that,
then you can learn anything.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:32.723
David out.

